fact WorkItem(id: "WI-262", description: some(value: "Type-level dot-access (field projection in type positions). Today dot-access is term-position-only: kernel-language.md §6.7 desugars 'term.field' to field_access(term, field) with two runtime modes (entity field, sort component). In type position — inside effects rows, requires/ensures type slots, parameterized-type bindings — dotted names are treated purely as qualified-name lookups (load.rs::remap_name joins segments and calls resolve_in_scope on the joined string). Consequence: 'effects Modify[result.a]', 'effects Modify[c.field]', 'effects Modify[s.backend]' all fail unless the symbol 'result.a' / 'c.field' / 's.backend' is pre-registered. WI-261 / proposal 041 shipped a narrow workaround for the named-tuple-return case: scan_operation_params pre-registers 'result.<field>' as a synthetic Param symbol per tuple field, so qualified-name lookup finds it. This does not generalize to parameters of entity / tuple type, where users will increasingly want to project ('Modify[c.backend]' for a Cell-typed param holding a backend, etc.). The infrastructure for type-level structure is already there: anthill.prelude.Type has arrow(param:, result:, effects:), named_tuple has fields by name, sorts have member operations and sub-sorts — reflection can answer 'what's the field f of type T?' for the cases that matter. What's missing is the language-side disambiguation rule and the typer's projection arm. Proposed direction: in type position, a dotted Name whose first segment resolves to a value-like name in the current scope (Param, the reserved 'result', a let-binding in scope, or any non-Sort/Namespace symbol) is parsed/resolved as projection over that value's type. The second-and-later segments are field names looked up structurally on the type. First-segment resolution to a Sort or Namespace continues to mean qualified-name (existing behavior). Open design: (1) parse-time vs load-time disambiguation — parse-time needs to distinguish but the parser may not have scope info; load-time keeps the parse uniform but means the resolver does both attempts. Probably load-time, mirroring how remap_name currently has a fallback path. (2) Multi-level projection 'result.a.b' — recursive field-access through the structure. (3) Interaction with type-parameter substitution (named-tuple field types may contain type vars that need to instantiate). (4) Whether positional tuple projection like 'result._1' should also work (probably yes, since named-tuple desugars positional to _1/_2/...). Drivers: WI-261 (proposal 041 — narrow workaround in place), proposal 027.1 (Modify[result.X] / Modify[result] for allocator-shaped ops), and any future feature that wants per-component effect attribution. Out of scope: pure-term-level field access (already works via §6.7). Acceptance: 'effects Modify[c.field]' typechecks for params c of entity / tuple type; 'effects Modify[result.a]' continues to work and uses the new projection path instead of the synthetic-symbol workaround; the workaround in scan_operation_params can be removed; cargo test green; existing stdlib parses unchanged."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-16T12:36:47Z"))
